# Session Documentation Requirement

This project maintains three living documents at the repository root. Update them **before
ending any session in which you changed code, configuration, or dependencies.** Do not wait
to be asked — treat it as part of finishing the work.

Skip the update only for read-only sessions where nothing changed.

## SESSION.md — always update

Prepend a new entry at the top, below the header, using the Entry Template at the bottom of
the file. Newest session first. Fill in every section; write "None" rather than deleting a
heading. The "What Was Done" section should carry enough detail — including approaches
tried and rejected — that someone returning in a month can reconstruct the reasoning
without reading the diff. Always state the status and working-tree state at the end.

If a previous entry says "In progress" or "Blocked" and this session resolved it, say so
explicitly in the new entry.

## ROLLBACK.md — update when you commit

Add a row for each new commit to the table for its date, creating the date section if
needed. Record the SHA, a description, the files touched, and the revert command. Update
the **Current HEAD** line and **Last updated** date at the top, and keep the
"Uncommitted Work" section at the bottom accurate.

Flag anything that does not revert cleanly:

- Schema or data changes in `init_db()` — reverting the code leaves the column and any
  rewritten rows in place.
- Changes to `_fit_and_save_pipeline()` — the model in Blob Storage must be retrained after
  a rollback.
- Commits that must be reverted as a group, or that are unsafe to revert at all.

## CONFLUENCE.md — update when behaviour or stack changes

Edit the affected section in place; this is a reference document, not a log. Update it when
you add or change a route, tab, category, environment variable, or database column; change
the model pipeline or PDF parsing behaviour; add or upgrade a dependency; or introduce a
constraint or gotcha a future developer would need to know. Bump the **Last updated** date.

Keep the API route table, database schema, environment variable table, and Known
Constraints list accurate — those are the sections people rely on most.
